    {'heading': 'Lesson XXI'},
    {'para': '**246. Declension of Consonant-stems, cont’d. Stems in palatals, etc.** 1. Final ⟨c⟩ of a stem reverts to the original guttural when it comes to stand as word-final, and before the *pada*-endings, becoming ⟨k⟩ when final, and before ⟨su⟩, and ⟨g⟩ before ⟨bh⟩. 2. Final ⟨j⟩ is oftenest treated exactly like ⟨c⟩ — for cases of other treatment, see below. 3. In the roots∗ ⟨diś⟩, ⟨dṛś⟩ and ⟨spṛś⟩, the ⟨ś⟩ is treated in the same way. 4. The ⟨s⟩ of ⟨su⟩ becomes ⟨ṣ⟩ after ⟨k⟩;'},
    {'para': '∗ In classical Sanskrit not many root-stems are used as independent substantives; but they are frequently employed, with adjective or (present) participial value, as final element of a compound word.'},
