    {'para': '**247.** 1. Final ⟨ś⟩ and ⟨ṣ⟩ of a stem regularly becomes the lingual mute (⟨ḍ⟩ or ⟨ṭ⟩) before ⟨bh⟩ and ⟨su⟩, and when word-final. For exceptions, see § 246, 3. 2. The final ⟨j⟩ of the root-stems ⟨rāj⟩, ‘rule’, ⟨yaj⟩, ‘sacrifice’, and ⟨sṛj⟩, with others; and 3. the final ⟨h⟩ of a number of roots, are treated like ⟨ś⟩ above. Thus, ⟨dviṣ⟩ m., ‘enemy’; ⟨viś⟩ m. pl., ‘people’, the ‘Vāiśya-caste’; ⟨lih⟩ m., f., (adj.) ‘licking’.'},
