    {'para': '**249.** 1. Nouns having the roots ⟨dah⟩, ‘burn’, and ⟨duh⟩, ‘milk’, ⟨druh⟩ ‘be hostile’, with others, as final element, and also ⟨uṣṇih⟩ f. (name of a certain metre), change the final ⟨h⟩ into ⟨k⟩ and ⟨g⟩. Thus, ⟨kāṣṭhadah⟩, ‘wood-burning’, makes nom.-voc. sing. ⟨kāṣṭhadhak⟩; ⟨kāmaduh⟩ f., ‘granting wishes’, nom.-voc. sing. ⟨kāmadhuk⟩, acc. ⟨-duham⟩, loc. pl. ⟨-dhukṣu⟩; ⟨mitradruh⟩ ‘friend-betraying,’ nom.-voc.-sing. ⟨mitradhruk⟩ etc. 2. In words with ⟨nah⟩, ‘bind,’ as final element, where ⟨h⟩ represents original ⟨dh⟩, the ⟨h⟩ becomes ⟨d⟩ and ⟨t⟩; thus, ⟨upānah⟩ f., ‘shoe, sandal,’ nom.-voc. sing. ⟨upānat⟩, acc. ⟨-naham⟩, instr. du. ⟨-nadbhyām⟩, loc. pl. ⟨-natsu⟩.'},
